<<<
vastly. In contexts of measure or comparison, where it means by much, by a great deal, as is vastly improved, a vastly larger audience, v. is still in regular use.
===
**vastly.** In contexts of measure or comparison, where it means by much, by a great deal, as *is vastly improved, a vastly larger audience*, *v.* is still in regular use.
>>>

<<<
great degree, as in I should vastly like to know, is vastly popular, it is an affectation; see WARDOUR STREET.
===
great degree, as in *I should vastly like to know, is vastly popular*, it is an affectation; see ^^WARDOUR STREET^^.
>>>

<<<
vaticinate makes -for; see -OR. The verb, formerly equivalent to prophesy, now usually connotes contempt, & means rather to play
